You can use the canvas context's arc() method to draw a circle:
```js
const canvas = document.querySelector('canvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

// Draw circle
ctx.arc(50, 50, 10, 0, 2 * Math.PI);
ctx.stroke();
```
This will draw a circle with radius 10 centered at (50, 50).
The arc() call takes:

X center point
Y center point
Radius
Start angle (0 = 3 o'clock position)
End angle (2*PI = full circle)

Then we call stroke() to actually draw the arc/circle.

There are different ways to scrape data from a website using JavaScript. One way is to use the Console panel of the browser’s developer tools to write JavaScript code that can access the HTML elements of the webpage. Another way is to use a JavaScript library such as Puppeteer or Cheerio that can automate browser actions and manipulate DOM elements.

Here is an example of a simple JS code that can scrape the title and author of each quote from https://quotes.toscrape.com/ using the Console panel:

```
// Select all div elements with class "quote"
let quotes = document.querySelectorAll("div.quote");

// Loop through each quote element
for (let quote of quotes) {
  // Get the text content of the span element with class "text"
  let text = quote.querySelector("span.text").textContent;
  // Get the text content of the small element with class "author"
  let author = quote.querySelector("small.author").textContent;
  // Print the text and author
  console.log(text + " - " + author);
}
```

